                <pb facs="1001" n="881" sameAs="frus:frus1922v01/pg881"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.77/2404: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Schurman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1922-02-02T15:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">February 2,
                                1922—3 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received 9:18 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>37. Department’s telegram no. 7, January 19, 4 p.m. I conferred immediately with
                    my British colleague. At that time he had not received his instructions. Later
                    we conferred several times. The instructions he received described in general
                    terms the plan suggested by the Department and did not express approval. The
                    Minister was asked to give his views on it. He replied to his Government that he
                    favored merely changing the existing arrangement by giving the Technical Board
                    somewhat greater powers. The British Government has not as yet answered his
                    telegram. …</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Schurman</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
